<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johan Laidoner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1884–1953</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johan Laidoner was an Estonian general who commanded the army in the War of Independence of 1918–1920. He again led the armed forces in 1924, when he suppressed a communist coup attempt, and from 1934, when he backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-pats">Konstantin Päts</a>'s authoritarian regime. Deported by the Soviet authorities in 1940, he died in Vladimir prison in 1953.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johan-laidoner.jpg" alt="Johan Laidoner" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author / J. &amp;amp; P. Parikas · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johan_Laidoner_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
